This Go version keeps separate rank and size data so either union method can be used. The root function uses path compression: after finding a root, it rewires the current node directly to it.

One detail that matters is the early return when both roots match. Without it, adding the size or rank again would corrupt the set information.

Dry run for nodes `2` and `3`: after joining `2` with `3`, both roots are the same, so `find(2, 3)` returns `true`. A node that has not been joined still has a different root.

```go
type DisjointSet struct {
    parent []int
    rank   []int
    size   []int
}

func NewDisjointSet(n int) *DisjointSet {
    ds := &DisjointSet{
        parent: make([]int, n),
        rank:   make([]int, n),
        size:   make([]int, n),
    }

    for i := 0; i < n; i++ {
        ds.parent[i] = i
        ds.size[i] = 1
    }

    return ds
}

func (ds *DisjointSet) root(node int) int {
    if ds.parent[node] != node {
        ds.parent[node] = ds.root(ds.parent[node])
    }
    return ds.parent[node]
}

func (ds *DisjointSet) find(u, v int) bool {
    return ds.root(u) == ds.root(v)
}

func (ds *DisjointSet) unionByRank(u, v int) {
    rootU := ds.root(u)
    rootV := ds.root(v)

    if rootU == rootV {
        return
    }

    if ds.rank[rootU] < ds.rank[rootV] {
        ds.parent[rootU] = rootV
    } else if ds.rank[rootU] > ds.rank[rootV] {
        ds.parent[rootV] = rootU
    } else {
        ds.parent[rootV] = rootU
        ds.rank[rootU]++
    }
}

func (ds *DisjointSet) unionBySize(u, v int) {
    rootU := ds.root(u)
    rootV := ds.root(v)

    if rootU == rootV {
        return
    }

    if ds.size[rootU] < ds.size[rootV] {
        ds.parent[rootU] = rootV
        ds.size[rootV] += ds.size[rootU]
    } else {
        ds.parent[rootV] = rootU
        ds.size[rootU] += ds.size[rootV]
    }
}
```

The constructor takes linear time. With path compression and either rank or size merging, later operations are amortized almost constant time, with `O(n)` stored space.

Here the root search is written with a loop instead of recursion. The first loop finds the root, and the second loop makes every visited node point closer to that root.

The `size` array keeps the number of nodes in each root's set. Smaller sets are attached to larger sets. The input values must be valid node indexes from `0` through `n - 1`.

